=====================================================
          GITHUB WORKFLOW CHEAT SHEET
=====================================================

1. git add .
   WHAT IT DOES:
   Stages all new, modified, or deleted files in your project folder.
   It prepares your changes to be saved, like gathering items into a box before sealing it.

2. git commit -m "Your descriptive message here"
   WHAT IT DOES:
   Creates a permanent local snapshot (save point) of all staged files on your computer.
   The message inside quotes describes what you changed (e.g., "Add Member class RegEx").

3. git push
   WHAT IT DOES:
   Uploads all your local saved commits from your computer to the remote GitHub repository.
   This makes your changes visible and accessible to your entire team online.

4. git pull
   WHAT IT DOES:
   Downloads and merges the latest code changes from the remote GitHub repository onto your computer.
   Always run this before you start coding to ensure you have your teammates' latest work.

=====================================================
              DAILY SAVE ROUTINE
=====================================================
Step 1: git pull                     (Sync with team before working)
Step 2: [Write your Java code]
Step 3: git add .                    (Stage changes)
Step 4: git commit -m "Your message" (Save locally)
Step 5: git push                     (Upload to GitHub)